<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Divine Comedy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1308–1321</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Commedia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Divine Comedy is a long narrative poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a>, written in the Florentine vernacular from about 1308 until his death in 1321. In three parts, Inferno, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a> and Paradiso, it describes the poet's journey through Hell, Purgatory and Heaven, guided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> and Beatrice, and is filled with figures from Florentine and Italian politics. It is regarded as the greatest work of Italian literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/divine-comedy.jpg" alt="Divine Comedy" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domenico di Michelino / After Alesso Baldovinetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dante_Domenico_di_Michelino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a>, who in general favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. Florence's Guelphs split around 1300 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-and-white-guelphs">White Guelphs</a> and the Black Guelphs, and the White Guelphs later supported greater imperial power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Dante was among the White Guelphs exiled in 1302 after troops under Charles of Valois entered the city at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>, who supported the Black Guelphs. The exile lasted the rest of his life and shows throughout the poem, from prophecies of his banishment to the damnation of some of his opponents.</p><p>The work was originally titled Comedìa. Dante used the word because poems in the ancient world were classed as high (tragedy) or low (comedy), and low poems had happy endings and everyday language. The adjective Divina first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a>'s Trattatello in laude di Dante, written between 1351 and 1355.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem has 14,233 lines in three cantiche, Inferno, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a> and Paradiso, each of 33 cantos, and an introductory canto brings the total to 100. Its verse form is terza rima, in tercets of eleven-syllable lines. The story is told in the first person and runs from the night before Good Friday to the Wednesday after Easter in the spring of 1300. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> guides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> through Hell and most of Purgatory, Beatrice from the end of Purgatorio, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> through the last cantos.</p><p>Hell has nine circles, Mount Purgatory nine rings crowned by the Garden of Eden, and Heaven nine celestial spheres beneath the Empyrean. Each punishment in Hell is a contrapasso, a symbolic instance of poetic justice: in Canto XX the fortune-tellers must walk with their heads turned backwards. Allegorically the poem represents the soul's journey towards God.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>According to the Italian Dante Society, no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in Dante's own hand survives, although some 800 copies from the 14th and 15th centuries are listed. Giovanni da Serravalle made the first complete translation, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> prose, in 1416, and Enrique de Villena completed a Castilian prose version in 1428. The first printed edition appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foligno">Foligno</a> on 11 April 1472, in 300 copies, of which fourteen survive.</p><p>The poem was recognised as a masterpiece in the centuries after it was written, largely fell into obscurity during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and was rediscovered in the English-speaking world by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> and the Romantic writers of the 19th century. Erich Auerbach held that it inaugurated literary realism in modern fiction. In 1919 Miguel Asín Palacios argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> drew on the Islamic tradition of Muhammad's night journey to heaven, but Francesco Gabrieli and other scholars have not found the claim convincing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Divine Comedy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dante’s <b>Divine Comedy</b>, together with the works of Petrarch and Giovanni Boccaccio, made the Tuscan dialect of Florence the basis of literary Italian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bible and its stories pervade European literature, from Dante’s <b>Divine Comedy</b> to Milton’s Paradise Lost.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars have suggested that it inspired a tercet in Dante's <b>Divine Comedy</b> (Paradiso XX:73–75), which also describes a lark in flight; others think Dante may have met the image through the poet Bondie Dietaiuti, or that a lark's flight was too common a sight for any link…</blockquote><a href="/songs/can-vei-la-lauzeta-mover">Can vei la lauzeta mover</a></li><li><blockquote>He never returned home, and wrote the '<b>Divine Comedy</b>' during his exile.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-27">27 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante died in Ravenna, months after finishing the <b>Divine Comedy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-14">14 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
